        <div id="biggest-gas" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Largest Gas-Only Generators
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Largest Gas-Only Models</h3>

          <p>
            For gasoline-only open frames, the Westinghouse WGen20000c sits at the top at 20,000 running watts, with the Generac GP18000EFI just behind at 18,000 running. The Generac is worth knowing about if you want a big gas portable with electronic fuel injection, which avoids the carburetor varnish that commonly keeps stored generators from starting. Generac lists it at 442.8 pounds, 816cc and 9 hours at 50% load from 14.2 gallons.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Case for Gas-Only</h3>

          <p>
            The case for gas-only is simplicity. There is no propane regulator, no fuel selector and no natural gas fitting to leak or fail. The case against is storage and supply. A generator this size uses roughly 1 to 2 gallons an hour, based on manufacturers' runtime ratings, and stored gasoline goes stale in months without stabilizer. During a regional outage, gas stations without power cannot pump.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Tri-Fuel Version Ranks Higher</h3>

          <p>
            That is why we rank the tri-fuel WGen20000TFc above the gas-only version on this page, even though they share an engine. The extra 16 pounds buys you two more fuels and a terminal block for a full-capacity connection. Unless you are certain you will never use propane or natural gas, the tri-fuel model is the better large generator.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You Go Gas-Only, Plan Fuel Storage</h3>

          <p>
            If you do go gas-only, put the money you save toward proper fuel storage: several approved cans, stabilizer, and a rotation schedule. Our <a href="/best-gas-generator/">best gas generators</a> roundup covers smaller gas units for comparison.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="biggest-dual-tri" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Largest Dual Fuel and Tri-Fuel Generators
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dual Fuel vs Tri-Fuel at the Top End</h3>

          <p>
            Dual fuel units run on gasoline and propane; tri-fuel units add natural gas. At the large end, the Westinghouse WGen20000TFc is the biggest tri-fuel portable we found, rated for 20,000 running watts on gasoline, 18,000 on propane and 16,000 on natural gas. Among dual fuel units, the DuroMax XP28000iH has the highest rating at 22,000 running on gas and 20,000 on propane, though it is an inverter. For a conventional open-frame dual fuel unit that one household can still manage, the DuroMax XP15000HX is rated at 12,000 running on gas and 11,400 on propane.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane Derating and Supply</h3>

          <p>
            Propane derating at this size is modest, usually 5 to 10%. The bigger issue is supply. A large engine empties a 20 lb grill cylinder in a few hours; Westinghouse rates the TFc at just 3 hours at 25% load on one. DuroMax recommends 60 lb tanks at minimum for its large units. Realistically, a large dual fuel generator needs 100 lb cylinders or a connection to a fixed tank. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-propane-does-a-generator-use/">how much propane a generator uses</a> gives the numbers by size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Natural Gas: Where Tri-Fuel Makes the Most Sense</h3>

          <p>
            Natural gas is where the largest tri-fuel units make the most sense. Plumbed to a home gas service, they run without refueling for as long as the gas flows. You lose about 20% of output and you need a licensed plumber and a meter that can supply the extra demand. At that point the setup is close to a standby installation, which we come back to below.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a wider look at fuel-flexible units at every size, see our <a href="/best-dual-fuel-generator/">best dual fuel generators</a> and <a href="/best-tri-fuel-generator/">best tri-fuel generators</a> roundups.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="biggest-inverter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Largest Inverter Generators
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the Largest Inverters Look Like</h3>

          <p>
            Searches for a large inverter generator usually picture a big version of a suitcase inverter. That is not what the largest inverters look like now. The DuroMax XP28000iH at 22,000 running watts and the XP16000iH at 13,000 are open-frame-style machines on wheels, weighing 466 and 368 pounds, with inverter electronics in place of a conventional alternator output.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Variable Engine Speed</h3>

          <p>
            What the inverter design gives you at this size is variable engine speed. Most of the time a home generator runs at well under half load, and an inverter lets the engine slow down to match. DuroMax lists the XP16000iH at 66.9 dB at 25% load and the XP28000iH at 70 dB, compared with 74 dBA for the Westinghouse open frames and 78 dB at 50% for the XP15000HX. Light-load fuel use is also lower: the XP28000iH is rated for 16 hours at 25% load from 12.7 gallons.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mid-Size Inverters You Can Move</h3>

          <p>
            Below these sit the mid-size inverters that people can actually move, such as the Champion 201423 at 9,000 running watts and 212.3 pounds. Its listed 64 dBA and under 3% THD make it the quietest and cleanest of the large units here. Our <a href="/best-inverter-generator/">best inverter generators</a> roundup covers the smaller end, and <a href="/inverter-generator-vs-regular-generator/">inverter vs regular generator</a> explains when the extra cost is worth it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Maturity Caveat</h3>

          <p>
            The caveat for the biggest inverters is maturity. Large open-frame engines have years of owner history, and small-engine shops know how to fix them. A 22kW inverter is a newer design, and if its inverter module fails, you are dependent on the manufacturer for parts. That is a reason to check warranty terms carefully; DuroMax lists 5 years residential on its large units.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="move-alone" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The Largest Generator You Can Move by Yourself
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Decides Whether One Person Can Move It</h3>

          <p>
            This is the category that matters most to the most buyers, and the one product listings never address. Weight alone does not decide it. Wheel size, handle design, balance and the surface all matter. But the pattern from owner reports is consistent: most adults can roll about 200 to 220 pounds on large wheels across a smooth driveway, and struggle beyond that, especially on grass, gravel or a slope.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Generator That Fits the Limit</h3>

          <p>
            The Champion 201423 fits that limit at 212.3 pounds, with 8-inch solid wheels and a compact 28-inch footprint. Its 9,000 running watts on gasoline is enough for a refrigerator, freezer, sump pump, lighting, a gas furnace and, in many houses, a central air system of up to about 3 tons. It has a 14-50R outlet, though Champion rates it at 37.5 amps, so it will not fill a full 50-amp inlet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Above 300 Pounds: Two People or a Fixed Spot</h3>

          <p>
            Above about 300 pounds, plan on two people or a permanent spot. The XP15000HX at 345 pounds is manageable for two adults on concrete. The 440 to 560-pound units are not realistic to move each outage for most households, and owners commonly report leaving them on a pad or trailer.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HONEST TAKE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If you live alone, the largest generator you should buy is the largest one you can move.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 20kW unit you cannot get out of the garage during an ice storm provides no power. A 9kW unit you can roll out in two minutes does.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Standby Unit Is the Answer</h3>

          <p>
            If you need more power than one person can manage but still want to handle it yourself, a standby unit is the answer, because it never needs moving. If you want power without moving anything heavy, <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">a portable power station for home backup</a> is the other option, though at far lower output.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outlets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Outlets and Connections on Big Generators
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The 14-50R Outlet</h3>

          <p>
            Large generators exist to power a house, and that means 240V outlets. The 14-50R is the receptacle that matters most. It is a 50-amp, 120/240V outlet, and one 14-50 cord can carry up to 12,000 watts. The L14-30R twist-lock carries up to 7,200 watts. Every unit on this page has at least one of each.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The 50-Amp Inlet Limit</h3>

          <p>
            The limit is easy to miss. A 20,000-watt generator connected through a standard 50-amp inlet still delivers only 12,000 watts to your panel. To use the full output of a WGen20000TFc, an electrician needs to wire its 125A terminal block to a 100-amp transfer switch or inlet. The XP28000iH and WGen20000c have two 14-50R outlets, but two cords cannot be combined into one inlet; the second outlet is for a separate load such as a shop, well pump or RV.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Outlet Ratings on Smaller Large Units</h3>

          <p>
            Smaller large units have their own details. The Champion 201423's 14-50R is rated at 37.5 amps, or 9,000 watts at 240V, which matches its running output. Our guides to <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch types and installation</a> and <a href="/best-manual-transfer-switch/">the best manual transfer switches</a> explain how to connect each size, and <a href="/best-240v-generator/">best 240V generators</a> covers the voltage question in more detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Connect Only Through a Transfer Switch or Interlock</h3>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, connect it to the house only through a transfer switch or a code-compliant interlock with an inlet box. Backfeeding through a dryer outlet with a double-male cord is illegal and can kill a line worker.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="standby-line" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Where the Largest Portables Meet Standby Generators
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Big Portables Start to Look Like Standby</h3>

          <p>
            Above about 12,000 running watts, the largest portables start to look like standby generators. They sit in one place, they need a heavy-duty electrical connection, and they run best on natural gas or a large propane tank. The difference is that a standby starts itself when the power fails and switches the house over automatically, while a portable needs you to wheel it out, connect it, start it and refuel it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Big Portable Makes Sense</h3>

          <p>
            A big portable makes sense when you also want to take it somewhere else, such as a job site, cabin or RV, when outages are rare, or when the up-front budget is tight. A standby makes more sense for long or frequent outages, for households with medical equipment, and for anyone who cannot safely move a 450-pound machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Compare Installed Totals</h3>

          <p>
            Compare installed totals, not sticker prices. A 20kW portable plus a 100-amp transfer switch, a gas connection and a pad can come close to the cost of an air-cooled standby. Our <a href="/best-whole-house-generator/">best whole house generators</a> roundup and <a href="/portable-vs-standby-generator/">portable vs standby generator</a> comparison help with that decision, and <a href="/whole-house-generator-installation-cost/">whole house generator installation cost</a> gives the typical installation price ranges.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Middle Ground</h3>

          <p>
            For many households the right answer is in the middle: a 9,000 to 12,000-watt portable connected through a 30 or 50-amp inlet to the circuits that matter. It is large enough for real backup and small enough to store and move. Our pillar guide to the <a href="/best-portable-generator/">best portable generators</a> covers that range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Owning a Large Generator: Storage and Upkeep
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Neglect Costs More on Big Units</h3>

          <p>
            Large generators need more care than small ones, and the consequences of neglect are worse. A 500-pound generator that will not start cannot easily be carried to a repair shop. Run it under load for 20 to 30 minutes every month, change the oil on the manufacturer's schedule, and keep the battery charged on electric-start models, because all of the big units rely on push-button or remote start.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fuel: The Most Common Failure</h3>

          <p>
            Fuel is the most common failure point. Use stabilizer in stored gasoline, or run the carburetor dry before storage. Electronic fuel injection on the Generac GP18000EFI reduces this problem. Running a dual or tri-fuel unit on propane for its monthly exercise is another good way to keep the gasoline side from varnishing, as long as you also run the gasoline occasionally.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Storage and Placement</h3>

          <p>
            Store it where it can roll straight to its run spot, and where it is protected from rain, flooding and theft. For outdoor placement during an outage, keep it at least 20 feet from the house with the exhaust pointed away; our guide on <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> covers the details, and <a href="/how-to-store-a-portable-generator/">how to store a portable generator</a> covers the off-season.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Planning for Noise</h3>

          <p>
            Finally, plan the noise. At 70 to 78 dB, the largest units are loud enough to bother neighbors at night. Inverters are quieter at light load, and positioning the exhaust away from the nearest homes makes a noticeable difference.
          </p>
        </div>

